export function Overview() {
  return <>
    <Note title="A framework with replaceable defaults">The diagrams and labs below illustrate the default SQLite/WAL state backend. SQLite, the shared object store, HTTP/log services, and additional guest-to-host capabilities have explicit extension interfaces. A standalone app such as <code>hello</code> needs no team namespace or repository workspace. See <code>docs/extensions/</code> for implementation contracts.</Note>
    <div className="hero-facts"><span><Icon name="cube" size={16}/> WebAssembly compute</span><span><Icon name="database" size={16}/> SQLite per actor</span><span><Icon name="shield" size={16}/> Object-store durability</span></div>
    <Onion/>
    <Section eyebrow="The 30-second version" title="Bring behavior to the state.">
      <p className="section-lead">Instead of loading an entity from a remote database for every operation, call an actor that owns a private database. StateX finds its current node, runs your method, and makes committed pages durable before acknowledging a write.</p>
      <Topology/>
      <div className="grid three"><Card title="One identity" icon="cube"><p><code>(app, type, key)</code> names the unit of isolation. A key creates an actor on first use; there is no per-actor route registration.</p></Card><Card title="One local transaction" icon="database"><p>Calls to the same actor are serialized. SQL updates and pending migrations share one host-controlled transaction.</p></Card><Card title="A portable home" icon="layers"><p>Resident memory and local disk can disappear. The next owner rebuilds the database from the shared object store.</p></Card></div>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Separate these ideas" title="Code, identity, and state are not the same thing.">
      <Flow label="App, type, actor identity, private database" nodes={[{ kicker: "APP", title: "demo/hello", text: "One deployed component" }, { kicker: "ACTOR TYPE", title: "counter", text: "One WIT interface" }, { kicker: "ACTOR KEY", title: "alice", text: "One logical identity" }, { kicker: "STATE", title: "SQLite", text: "One private database", tone: "mint" }]}/>
      <Code title="Generated Python client / conceptual example">{`app = HelloApp("http://any-node:9876")\napp.counter("alice").increment(by=1)\n# POST /v1/apps/demo/hello/actors/counter/alice/increment\n# The actual generated class name follows the app's name.`}</Code>
      <Note title="Durable state is SQLite, not the whole Wasm process">The component instance is resident and reusable, but guest memory is not checkpointed to the store. Persist anything you need after eviction, a code update, or a node crash.</Note>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="What this is / what this is not" title="A focused execution engine, not every distributed system.">
      <div className="grid two"><Card title="What the engine supplies" icon="check" tone="mint"><p>Actor identity, request routing, serialized execution, private SQLite, object-store replication, ownership coordination, typed actor calls, and component deployment.</p></Card><Card title="What your application supplies" icon="branch"><p>Idempotency, cross-actor protocols, external-effect coordination, business authorization, schedulers, durable work delivery, and application-specific retry policies.</p></Card></div>
      <Note title="Read the pictures as a protocol, not a formal proof" tone="amber">The documented guarantees assume working conditional writes, read/list consistency, bounded clock skew, and correct operational configuration. The failure and reference chapters call out the exact implementation boundaries.</Note>
      <Source files="README.md; docs/architecture.md; docs/guarantees.md"/>
    </Section>
  </>;
}

