function ComponentExplorer() {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(0);
  const c = components[selected];
  return <div className="component-explorer"><div className="component-list" aria-label="Select a component">{components.map((item, i) => <button key={item.name} aria-pressed={selected === i} onClick={() => setSelected(i)}><Icon name={item.icon}/><span>{item.name}</span><span className="component-index">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span></button>)}</div><div className="component-detail" aria-live="polite"><Badge>COMPONENT {selected + 1} / {components.length}</Badge><h3>{c.name}</h3><p className="big-copy">{c.role}</p><div className="io-row"><div><span className="mini-label">Receives</span><p>{c.input}</p></div><Icon name="arrow"/><div><span className="mini-label">Produces</span><p>{c.output}</p></div></div><p>{c.details}</p><Note title="Boundary" tone="amber">{c.boundary}</Note><Source files={c.file}/></div></div>;
}

export function Architecture() {
  return <>
    <Topology/>
    <Section eyebrow="Component atlas" title="Click a boundary. See its contract."><ComponentExplorer/></Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Inside one node" title="Many actors; independent serial lanes.">
      <div className="node-anatomy"><div className="node-header"><Icon name="cube"/><strong>One node process</strong><Badge>one shared node lease</Badge></div><div className="grid three">{["alice", "bob", "checkout"].map((name, i) => <div className="actor-lane" key={name}><div className="mini-label">Actor slot {i + 1}</div><h3>{name}</h3><div className="queue-dots"><span/><span/><span/><Icon name="arrow" size={18}/><Icon name="shield" size={20}/></div><p>slot mutex</p><Flow vertical nodes={[{ title: "Wasm instance", text: "Resident, not durable" }, { title: "SQLite + WAL", text: "Private to this actor", tone: "mint" }]}/></div>)}</div><div className="node-bottom">Shared app code cache · peer client · runtime engine · lease renewal · deploy polling · idle eviction</div></div>
      <Note title="Serial does not mean a built-in message queue">Concurrent callers wait on an in-memory slot mutex. There is no persisted inbox, durable buffering, or automatic background execution in this mechanism. A request lost before durable application still needs a client or driver to retry.</Note>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Two kinds of traffic" title="The store is both coordinator and recovery medium.">
      <div className="grid two"><Card title="Coordination & configuration" icon="clock" tone="mint"><p><code>nodes/</code> leases identify live sessions. <code>owner.json</code> points to the current actor owner. <code>deploy/</code> publishes code versions. <code>fleet/peer-auth.json</code> supplies peer signing material.</p></Card><Card title="Durable actor data" icon="database" tone="lavender"><p><code>ltx/e&lt;epoch&gt;/</code> contains snapshots and transaction segments. These are used for restore and compaction, not queried as application records during every SQL read.</p></Card></div>
      <Table headers={["Question", "Source of truth", "Not this"]} rows={[["Where should a call run?", "owner record + matching live node session", "A consistent-hash ring or client-side shard map"], ["Which app code should a node load?", "deploy/<app>/current.json", "Per-actor route registration"], ["What survives lost local disk?", "Snapshot + contiguous segments", "Wasm memory or local SQLite alone"], ["Who wins competing activation?", "If-Match / If-None-Match conditional write", "A last-writer-wins unconditional PUT"]]}/>
      <Source files="crates/node/src/node.rs:29-131, 293-431; crates/node/src/lib.rs:79-156"/>
    </Section>
  </>;
}

export function Journey() {
  return <>
    <RequestStepper/>
    <Section eyebrow="A gate, not a return statement" title="Three things must line up for a write reply.">
      <div className="ack-equation"><div><Icon name="layers"/><strong>Segment uploaded</strong><small>committed pages in the store</small></div><span>+</span><div><Icon name="clock"/><strong>Lease valid</strong><small>inside the local safety margin</small></div><span>+</span><div><Icon name="shield"/><strong>Still the owner</strong><small>owned + node + session + epoch</small></div><span>=</span><div className="ack-success"><strong>ACK</strong><small>successful write response</small></div></div>
      <p>A method return is only the input to the host's decision. Local SQLite commit happens before object-store upload. Failure between these steps can make the client's outcome uncertain; the engine drops the resident actor rather than continuing on potentially ambiguous local state.</p>
      <Note title="Exact source behavior: the read path is different" tone="amber">The post-upload lease and owner checks run only when WAL capture produces a segment. A genuinely no-write call skips that branch. The entry lease check is not a formal guarantee of linearizable reads across arbitrary delays. Also, the write branch checks lease validity before awaiting the owner GET; it does not perform a second lease check after that GET.</Note>
      <Source files="crates/node/src/node.rs:328-335, 434-518"/>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Take the cold path" title="Activation happens before the first method.">
      <Flow nodes={[{ title: "Acquire", text: "Win owner.json CAS" }, { title: "Restore", text: "Snapshot + contiguous log" }, { title: "Open DB", text: "WAL / FULL sync" }, { title: "Snapshot", text: "Publish new epoch base", tone: "mint" }, { title: "Invoke", text: "Run first call" }]}/>
      <div className="grid two"><Card title="New or deleted actor" icon="cube"><p>Start fresh, using txid 0. A deleted record still retains the last epoch; recreation increments it. Initial activation uploads a baseline snapshot before lazy migrations and the first method.</p></Card><Card title="Previously existing actor" icon="database"><p>Clear the local replica directory. Find the newest usable epoch, download its newest snapshot, apply contiguous later segments, then checkpoint and upload that state into the new epoch.</p></Card></div>
      <Detail title="Why activation needs its own snapshot"><p>A new epoch becomes independent of older prefixes. Later stale writes to an old epoch cannot alter the selected new epoch's database image. An epoch with no snapshot is not a usable restore base; recovery can fall back to an older one.</p></Detail>
      <Detail title="What the lock actually covers"><p>The actor's slot mutex prevents concurrent local calls. It stays held while execution runs on a blocking thread and while pages are uploaded. If compaction is triggered, a background task retains the lock through checkpoint, snapshot upload, and garbage collection. A reply can already be returned while the next call waits for that task.</p></Detail>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Follow the response shape" title="Domain errors are different from execution failures.">
      <div className="grid two"><Code title="Successful increment">{`HTTP 200\n{"result": 42}`}</Code><Code title="Method returns err(E)">{`HTTP 422\n{"error": {\n  "code": "method_error",\n  "message": "method returned an error",\n  "detail": "insufficient-funds"\n}}`}</Code></div>
      <p>A successful top-level WIT <code>result&lt;T,E&gt;</code> is unwrapped into the HTTP result. A method-level error rolls back the actor transaction. Nested result values still use their own WIT-to-JSON representation.</p>
      <ChapterLink id="failures">Compare every failure window</ChapterLink>
    </Section>
  </>;
}

export function Leases() {
  return <>
    <div className="grid two"><Card title="Node lease: am I alive?" icon="clock" tone="mint"><p><code>nodes/&lt;id&gt;.json</code></p><p>One per node process. Contains node ID, random session, advertised internal URL, start time, and expiry. Renewed by conditional overwrite, normally every TTL / 3.</p></Card><Card title="Actor record: who owns this?" icon="shield" tone="lavender"><p><code>actors/&lt;app&gt;/&lt;type&gt;/&lt;key&gt;/owner.json</code></p><p>One per actor identity. Contains node, session, epoch, and owned / unowned / deleted state. It points to a node lease; it does not carry a separate actor TTL.</p></Card></div>
    <Section eyebrow="Time is part of the protocol" title="A safety gap separates old and new owners."><LeaseLab/></Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Versions, not wishful thinking" title="Two nodes can race. Only one can replace the record."><CasRace/><Source files="crates/node/src/owner.rs:56-96"/></Section>
    <Section eyebrow="State transitions" title="The actor record is never erased.">
      <Flow label="Actor ownership lifecycle" nodes={[{ title: "Missing", text: "No owner record yet" }, { title: "Owned · e1", text: "Conditional create", tone: "mint" }, { title: "Unowned · e1", text: "Idle or graceful release" }, { title: "Owned · e2", text: "Next touch + CAS", tone: "mint" }]}/>
      <Flow label="Deletion and recreation lifecycle" nodes={[{ title: "Owned · e2", text: "Existing database" }, { title: "Deleted · e2", text: "Conditional tombstone", tone: "amber" }, { title: "Owned · e3", text: "Next call creates fresh", tone: "mint" }]}/>
      <Table headers={["Situation", "Protocol response", "Why it matters"]} rows={[["Live owner, same session", "Forward to its advertise URL.", "The receiving node does not steal a busy actor."], ["Same node ID, new session", "Old owner's session no longer matches; takeover can be attempted.", "A restart is not the same process incarnation."], ["Expired / absent node lease", "CAS owner record to new node/session and epoch + 1.", "A node's lease expiry does not itself execute an actor."], ["CAS precondition fails", "Re-read; forward or try again based on the new record.", "The loser cannot assume ownership."], ["Idle release", "Keep epoch, set unowned, drop local actor.", "Next activation restores state with a higher epoch."], ["Delete", "CAS deleted, then attempt data cleanup.", "A tombstone preserves epoch monotonicity; subsequent use starts fresh."]]}/>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="What fencing means" title="Stop trusting the old process.">
      <div className="grid three"><Card title="Renewal rejected" icon="alert"><p>A clean precondition rejection means the lease record changed. The node fences itself immediately when the renewal result is observed.</p></Card><Card title="Renewal too late" icon="clock"><p>Other store failures log a warning. If the node cannot renew inside the local deadline, the loop fences it. Requests cannot pass valid() after the cutoff.</p></Card><Card title="Drop & restart" icon="shield"><p>The node signals fencing and drops available resident slots. In production, <code>statex node</code> exits with code 3; a supervisor should restart it.</p></Card></div>
      <Note title="Fencing is not an undo operation" tone="amber">It cannot unsend HTTP, undo a separately committed callee, or promise that every in-flight function has synchronously stopped. Busy slots are not immediately cleared by try_lock-based drop_all; production process exit and per-call acknowledgement checks are important parts of the operational design.</Note>
      <Detail title="Who measures expiry, and when?"><p>Renewal computes the advertised expiry from the time before sending its store request, not from the response time. The local predicate is <code>now + max(TTL/5, 200ms) &lt; valid_until</code>. Peers use the stored expiry, matching the owner record's session. Keep node clocks aligned; making TTL tiny is not a free reduction in failover latency.</p></Detail>
      <Detail title="Graceful shutdown versus crash"><p>A graceful shutdown attempts actor release and then marks the node lease expired. This avoids waiting out a healthy TTL when store operations succeed. A crash cannot release anything; other nodes initially see the old lease as live, then compete after expiry. Neither path proactively migrates an actor without a request.</p></Detail>
      <Source files="crates/node/src/lease.rs; crates/node/src/owner.rs; crates/node/src/node.rs:585-611; crates/node/src/lib.rs:29-56, 95-107"/>
    </Section>
  </>;
}

export function Durability() {
  return <>
    <div className="grid two"><Card title="Local plane" icon="database"><p>Wasm instance → SQLite connection → database file + WAL. Fast, private to an actor, disposable after the durable log is published.</p><Badge tone="amber">NOT THE RECOVERY AUTHORITY</Badge></Card><Card title="Shared plane" icon="layers" tone="mint"><p>Object store → epoch snapshot → later transaction segments. Sufficient to reconstruct acknowledged database state under the documented assumptions.</p><Badge tone="green">DURABLE RECOVERY INPUT</Badge></Card></div>
    <Section eyebrow="From mutation to pages" title="Replicate the result, not a rerun of the method.">
      <Flow nodes={[{ title: "SQLite COMMIT", text: "WAL has committed frames" }, { title: "WalTail capture", text: "Final changed page images" }, { title: "Encode segment", text: "Metadata + pages + CRC32" }, { title: "Object PUT", text: "One txid in one epoch", tone: "mint" }]}/>
      <div className="page-demo" aria-label="Changed page illustration"><div><span className="mini-label">Database pages</span><div className="page-grid">{Array.from({ length: 12 }, (_, i) => <span key={i} className={[1, 4, 8].includes(i) ? "changed" : ""}>p{i + 1}</span>)}</div></div><Icon name="arrow" size={30}/><div><span className="mini-label">This transaction's segment</span><div className="page-grid changed-pages">{[2, 5, 9].map(n => <span className="changed" key={n}>p{n}</span>)}</div><small>Only final images of changed pages</small></div></div>
      <Detail title="The WAL capture algorithm"><p>Read the 32-byte WAL header and 24-byte frame headers. A salt change or restarted file resets the cursor. Accumulate page images until a commit-size marker appears; keep the final image for each page number. Ignore uncommitted trailing frames and pages beyond the committed database size. Capture advances the offset only through committed data.</p></Detail>
      <Detail title="What is in a .ltx segment?"><p>The custom binary codec starts with <code>STXLTX01</code>, then epoch, txid, page size, final database page count, and changed-page count. Each page entry has its number and bytes. CRC32 detects accidental corruption; it is not a cryptographic authenticity guarantee. Replay writes pages at their file offsets, resizes the database to its final page count, and syncs it.</p></Detail>
      <Source files="crates/ltx/src/lib.rs:18-194"/>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Recovery is selective" title="Newest usable epoch. Newest snapshot. No gaps."><RestoreLab/></Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Keep recovery bounded" title="Compaction trades many deltas for one image.">
      <Flow nodes={[{ title: "64 segments", text: "Default snapshot interval" }, { title: "TRUNCATE checkpoint", text: "Fold WAL into SQLite" }, { title: "Stream snapshot", text: "Hold actor slot lock", tone: "mint" }, { title: "Garbage collect", text: "Delete superseded history" }]}/>
      <div className="grid two"><Card title="Publish before deleting" icon="shield"><p>After the new snapshot is durable, delete older epochs, older snapshots in the current epoch, and segments at or below the snapshot txid. Never treat these objects as an indefinite audit log or a built-in point-in-time backup history.</p></Card><Card title="Stream, do not buffer the whole DB" icon="database"><p>Azure file uploads use 8 MiB blocks followed by a block-list commit. Downloads use 8 MiB range reads pinned to one ETag. The slot lock keeps the checkpointed database file stable through upload.</p></Card></div>
      <Note title="Compaction failures do not erase the already uploaded transaction">Compaction happens after the acknowledgement gate and can outlive the response. Upload/GC failures log warnings and may leave redundant objects. A checkpoint failure drops the actor. The durable segment chain remains the recovery path when no newer usable snapshot was published.</Note>
      <Source files="crates/node/src/actor.rs:204-261; crates/node/src/node.rs:480-510; crates/store/src/azure.rs"/>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Bucket anatomy" title="The namespace tells the story.">
      <Code title="Illustrative object-store keys">{`fleet/peer-auth.json\nnodes/node-a.json\ndeploy/demo.hello/current.json\ndeploy/demo.hello/<content-id>/component.wasm\ndeploy/demo.hello/<content-id>/manifest.json\nactors/demo.hello/counter/alice/owner.json\nactors/demo.hello/counter/alice/ltx/e0000000008/\n  snapshot-0000000000000042.db\n  0000000000000043.ltx\n  0000000000000044.ltx`}</Code>
      <p><code>demo/hello</code> becomes <code>demo.hello</code> in storage. App segments cannot contain dots, so this mapping does not collide with a literal dotted app name. Epoch and transaction numbers are distinct: an activation increments the epoch; a committed page-producing transaction increments txid.</p>
      <Table headers={["Store operation", "Protocol use"]} rows={[["get / get_range / list", "Read leases, ownership, code and restore objects; enumerate consistent history."], ["put_if_absent", "Create a missing lease, owner record, or deployment pointer."], ["put_if_match(etag)", "Renew, acquire, release, delete logically, or publish only if the observed version still matches."], ["put / put_file", "Publish segment bytes or a streamed snapshot."], ["get_to_file", "Restore a snapshot without keeping the whole image in memory."], ["delete", "Remove data superseded by a durable snapshot or attempt cleanup after actor deletion."]]}/>
      <Note title="Run the conformance probe, but know its scope" tone="amber"><code>statex diagnose</code> checks conditional create/update, stale-version rejection, ranged reads, and list-after-write. Passing a small probe is necessary evidence of store behavior, not an exhaustive distributed consistency or durability proof.</Note>
      <Source files="crates/node/src/layout.rs; crates/store/src/lib.rs:1-66, 113-149"/>
    </Section>
  </>;
}

export function Runtime() {
  return <>
    <div className="sandbox"><div className="sandbox-header"><Icon name="shield"/><span>WASMTIME COMPONENT SANDBOX</span><Badge>one instance per resident actor</Badge></div><div className="sandbox-core"><Icon name="cube" size={38}/><div><h3>Your actor implementation</h3><p>Rust or Python → WASI p2 component → exported WIT methods</p></div></div><div className="sandbox-ports">{[["context", "Identity + epoch"], ["sql", "Private SQLite"], ["http-client", "Allowed hosts"], ["log", "Node logs"], ["actors", "Typed calls"]].map(([name, desc]) => <div key={name}><span className="port-dot"/><strong>{name}</strong><small>{desc}</small></div>)}</div><div className="sandbox-floor">No preopened directories · no inherited environment · no granted raw sockets</div></div>
    <Section eyebrow="Useful doors" title="Every host capability has a boundary.">
      <Table headers={["Import", "What the guest can do", "Boundary / detail"]} rows={[["context", "Read app, actor type, key, epoch.", "Use identity to address your entity; epoch is not a business version."], ["sql", "Execute statements; query rows and typed values.", "The host owns transaction control. BEGIN, COMMIT, SAVEPOINT, ATTACH, VACUUM, PRAGMA and related controls are rejected."], ["http-client", "Send HTTP(S) through the host.", "Host allowlist is explicit; side effects are not transactional. Response body cap is 10 MiB."], ["log", "Emit structured-level messages to node logs.", "Logs are observability, not a durable application ledger."], ["actors / generated client imports", "Call another actor with typed arguments.", "Calls go through the node; callee commits independently and can return transport/runtime call-error."], ["WASI p2", "Use the configured component runtime facilities.", "No automatic grant of local directories, environment, or raw socket access; stdout/stderr go to node logs."]]}/>
      <div className="metrics three"><div><strong>5,000 ms</strong><span>default method timeout</span></div><div><strong>64 MiB</strong><span>default guest memory limit</span></div><div><strong>10 ms</strong><span>engine epoch tick</span></div></div>
      <Note title="Execution limits are not an end-to-end API latency bound">Wasmtime epoch interruption bounds guest execution; slot waiting, activation, store I/O, forwarding, and host calls have their own timing behavior. A long queue or cold restore is not included in a simple “5 seconds per HTTP request” promise.</Note>
      <Source files="crates/runtime/src/lib.rs:22-45, 95-174, 229-238; crates/runtime/src/host.rs:72-181; wit/statex-host.wit"/>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="The contract is executable" title="WIT is the common language.">
      <div className="grid two"><Code title="Illustrative actor interface">{`package demo:hello;\n\ninterface counter {\n  increment: func(by: s64) -> s64;\n  get: func() -> s64;\n}\n\nworld app {\n  import statex:host/sql;\n  export counter;\n}`}</Code><div><h3>From interface to invocation</h3><p>Each named exported interface becomes an actor type. Its functions become callable methods. The manifest holds this schema alongside migrations, HTTP policy, memory/time limits, and typed call imports.</p><p>Top-level world functions and resource-based actor interfaces are not supported. Other component toolchains can work if they emit the supported WASI p2 component and WIT surface.</p><Badge>NO MANUAL ROUTE REGISTRATION</Badge></div></div>
      <Table headers={["WIT shape", "JSON representation", "Important detail"]} rows={[["record", "{\"display-name\": \"Ada\"}", "Input lookup also accepts snake_case and camelCase spellings."], ["option<T>", "null or a T value", "Omitted optional fields/arguments can map to none."], ["enum", "\"ready\"", "Variant names are strings."], ["variant", "{\"tag\": \"ready\", \"value\": ...}", "A payload-free variant can also be supplied as a bare tag string."], ["list<u8>", "Base64 string", "Other lists are JSON arrays."], ["s64 / u64", "JSON number or numeric string on input", "Use strings where JavaScript integer precision would be lost."], ["nested result<T,E>", "{\"ok\": ...} or {\"err\": ...}", "Top-level method result is separately unwrapped into HTTP 200 or 422."], ["function arguments", "{\"by\": 1} or [1]", "No-argument calls can have an empty body; names and order come from WIT."]]}/>
      <Source files="crates/runtime/src/manifest.rs:159-223, 329-418; crates/runtime/src/json.rs"/>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Actors can call actors" title="Typed calls. Separate transactions.">
      <Flow label="Nested actor call with independent commits" nodes={[{ title: "Actor A", text: "Transaction + slot held" }, { title: "Client import", text: "Key first; WIT → JSON" }, { title: "NodeCaller", text: "Local invoke or forward" }, { title: "Actor B", text: "Own commit + durability", tone: "mint" }, { title: "A resumes", text: "Can still roll back", tone: "amber" }]}/>
      <div className="grid two"><Code title="Two layers of result / conceptual type">{`result<\n  result<receipt, payment-error>,\n  statex:host/actors.call-error\n>\n\nouter error = transport / runtime / routing\ninner error = callee's business result`}</Code><div><h3>Why the chain travels with the call</h3><p>The caller adds itself to the chain. The node rejects a target already on that chain, or a chain with 16 entries, with <code>508 cycle</code>. Waiting is bounded by the caller's remaining deadline.</p><p>Two independent chains can still wait on each other's locks: A → B and B → A. That case is not detected by the per-chain check; timeout breaks the wait.</p></div></div>
      <Note title="A callee's commit cannot be rolled back by its caller" tone="amber">If B succeeds, then A traps, B's state remains. A callee timeout/unavailable is also an unknown outcome. Retrying safely requires stable step IDs or idempotency keys in the callee, not just a catch-and-retry loop in A.</Note>
      <Detail title="HTTP policy and external side effects"><p>The host allowlist accepts exact hosts, wildcard subdomains such as <code>*.example.com</code>, or <code>*</code>. It is host-based, not method/path-based. The default HTTP adapter disables redirects and bounds response size; replacement transports must honor admission and deadlines. Do not equate an allowlist with a complete network-isolation policy. Keep the list narrow and treat each request as an external effect that may survive an actor rollback. A durable intent table plus an external relay is an application design, not a built-in atomic HTTP feature.</p></Detail>
      <Detail title="Instance reuse is not state rollback"><p>The actor keeps its Wasmtime instance between calls. A trap drops the instance, and switching app code resets it. SQLite rollback does not rewind arbitrary guest memory. Prefer SQLite for durable or transaction-sensitive state rather than in-memory counters.</p></Detail>
      <Source files="crates/runtime/src/calls.rs:91-162; crates/node/src/node.rs:138-160, 349-362; crates/node/src/actor.rs:146-202"/>
    </Section>
  </>;
}

const failureRows = [
  ["Owner crashes", "Old node stops responding; its stored lease may still look alive.", "Wait for lease expiry, CAS a new epoch, restore, activate.", "Acknowledged writes survive under the documented assumptions; requests in flight have uncertain outcomes."],
  ["Node loses store access", "Renewal/upload/owner reads can fail.", "Local lease validity closes; renewal loop fences; writes cannot pass the full acknowledgement path.", "A failed store request may have applied remotely. Treat upload/ack failure as unknown."],
  ["Node cannot reach owner", "Forwarding transport fails while owner may still be healthy.", "Retry routing; do not take over a matching live session.", "Reachability from one node is not ownership permission."],
  ["Two takeover attempts", "Both readers observe the same old ETag.", "One conditional write wins; the loser re-reads.", "Only the winner proceeds with that acquired record."],
  ["Stale actor owner", "Lease/owner identity no longer matches.", "No write acknowledgement; discard local actor.", "Old-epoch objects do not replace the newer snapshot epoch."],
  ["Guest method err / trap", "Domain error, panic, deadline interruption, or guest memory exhaustion.", "Rollback local SQL; method err is 422, trap is 500; trap resets instance.", "External HTTP and independently committed callees are not rolled back."],
  ["Restore object missing or corrupt", "A listed snapshot/segment disappears, or segment decoding fails.", "Activation fails; routing retries may eventually surface 503.", "Do not silently fabricate a successful actor restore."],
  ["Compaction upload/GC fails", "A snapshot task logs failure after the write gate.", "Keep durable history; retry compaction on later writes as applicable.", "The write reply is not a guarantee that compaction or old-object deletion finished."],
  ["Reply lost", "Server completed, but the client did not receive the response.", "Client retries by stable request ID and reads the stored result.", "Exactly-once client observation is not built in."],
  ["New deployment fails to load", "Refresh loop logs an error.", "The node retains previously loaded app state until a successful refresh.", "Deployment visibility is polled, not an atomic fleet-wide code switch."]
];

export function Failures() {
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState("");
  const rows = failureRows.filter(r => r.join(" ").toLowerCase().includes(filter.toLowerCase()));
  return <>
    <FailureLab/>
    <Section eyebrow="Design for ambiguity" title="Unknown is a real outcome, not an error synonym.">
      <div className="grid three"><Card title="Committed?" icon="database"><p>Did SQLite commit on the current node? That alone says nothing about surviving the loss of local disk.</p></Card><Card title="Durable?" icon="layers"><p>Did the segment reach the store? It might have, even if the upload response or later ownership read failed.</p></Card><Card title="Observed?" icon="check"><p>Did the client receive success? A reply can be lost after all server-side work completed.</p></Card></div>
      <Code title="Application-level idempotency / illustrative SQL and protocol">{`CREATE TABLE requests (\n  request_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  result_json TEXT NOT NULL\n);\n\n# In the SAME actor call transaction:\n# 1. If request_id exists, return the recorded result.\n# 2. Apply the business mutation.\n# 3. Insert request_id + the exact result to return.\n# The host commits and replicates both together.`}</Code>
      <p>Choose keys at the logical-operation level, keep them stable across retries, define a retention policy, and bind them to the expected operation/payload so the same key cannot accidentally refer to different work. A uniqueness constraint prevents duplicate local application; an external service needs its own matching protection.</p>
      <Note title="The Python client already retries by default" tone="amber">The transport defaults to 3 retries (up to 4 attempts), with exponential backoff starting at 0.2 seconds. It retries an HTTP error whose code is <code>unavailable</code> and URL/network errors; it does not inspect whether the method is idempotent. A non-idempotent increment can therefore be repeated without an explicit retry loop in your application.</Note>
      <Source files="sdk/python/statex_client/_runtime.py:55-89"/>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Failure matrix" title="What breaks, what reacts, what remains.">
      <label className="search-field"><Icon name="search" size={18}/><input aria-label="Filter failure conditions" placeholder="Filter failures, e.g. owner, store, deployment..." value={filter} onChange={e => setFilter(e.target.value)}/></label>
      <Table headers={["Failure condition", "What happens", "Engine response", "Application meaning"]} rows={rows}/>
      {!rows.length && <p role="status">No matching failure conditions. Clear the filter to see all cases.</p>}
      <Source files="docs/guarantees.md; crates/node/src/{node,lease,actor,lib}.rs"/>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Know the response" title="A compact error contract."><Table headers={["HTTP", "Code / outcome", "Meaning", "Response strategy"]} rows={errors}/></Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Timing and availability" title="Failover is not a zero-downtime promise.">
      <div className="grid two"><Card title="Lease waiting" icon="clock"><p>Default TTL is 10 seconds. Immediately after a crash, a still-live-looking owner may be unreachable. Takeover eligibility follows lease expiry; cold restore adds more time.</p></Card><Card title="Retry budget is not a stopwatch" icon="branch"><p>The routing loop permits retries for roughly 2 × TTL, sleeping 250 ms between attempts. A forwarded HTTP call itself has a 60-second client timeout; an in-flight attempt is not cut off at the retry-loop deadline.</p></Card></div>
      <Note title="Do not promise a universal 20-second response ceiling" tone="amber">The 2 × TTL value controls whether another retry is scheduled, not an absolute end-to-end deadline. Lock acquisition, component work, and storage/network I/O can extend latency. Actor-to-actor callers separately bound their wait by the caller's remaining deadline.</Note>
      <Source files="crates/node/src/node.rs:214-216, 309-325, 544-563; docs/guarantees.md"/>
    </Section>
  </>;
}

export function Development() {
  return <>
    <Flow nodes={[{ title: "Define", text: "WIT + migrations" }, { title: "Implement", text: "Rust / Python" }, { title: "Verify", text: "Schema + sandbox" }, { title: "Publish", text: "Objects + CAS", tone: "mint" }, { title: "Adopt", text: "Poll, next call" }]}/>
    <Section eyebrow="Authoring surfaces" title="A small set of files defines an app.">
      <div className="grid two"><Code title="Typical workspace app">{`apps/demo/hello/\n  statex.toml\n  wit/app.wit\n  wit/deps/                 # host + client contracts\n  migrations/counter/\n    0001_init.sql\n    0002_add_history.sql\n  src/lib.rs               # Rust implementation\n  # or app.py for a Python app`}</Code><div><Table headers={["File", "Purpose"]} rows={[["statex.toml", "App identity, component build metadata, limits, HTTP policy and calls."], ["wit/app.wit", "Typed imported capabilities and exported actor APIs."], ["migrations/<type>/*.sql", "Ordered schema history for that actor type."], ["Implementation", "Business methods using host SQL, typed actors, logging and HTTP."], ["Generated bindings", "Client interfaces and language-specific wrappers, derived from WIT."]]}/></div></div>
      <Code title="Standalone workflow / commands to run yourself">{`export STATEX_GUEST_PATH=/path/to/statex/crates/guest\nstatex new hello                  # standalone Rust app\n# statex new --lang python hello\ncd hello\ncargo test                        # native guest tests (Rust)\nstatex dev                        # local node + rebuild loop\nstatex call counter alice increment -a by=2\nstatex codegen python              # typed external client\nstatex check\nstatex diagnose                   # configured store conformance\nstatex deploy                     # verify, upload, publish\n# Optional workspaces support check --all and registry build.`}</Code>
      <p>These commands describe the repository workflow; this guide does not execute them. For Python guest applications, the CLI builds components with componentize-py and supports native test stubs via <code>statex test</code>. Any additional tools should be installed through your approved development process.</p>
      <Source files="README.md; docs/developer-guide.md; crates/cli/src/{scaffold,project,python,codegen}.rs"/>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Publish code without rebuilding the fleet" title="The pointer is the deployment switch.">
      <Flow nodes={[{ title: "Build & verify", text: "Instantiate with migrated DBs" }, { title: "Upload content", text: "component.wasm + manifest" }, { title: "Compare & CAS", text: "current.json version", tone: "mint" }, { title: "Node polls", text: "2-second default" }, { title: "Next actor call", text: "New instance + lazy migration" }]}/>
      <div className="grid two"><Card title="Compatibility is part of publishing" icon="shield"><p>The deploy path compares the new manifest to the deployed one inside the current-pointer CAS loop. It checks typed calls against deployed callees and checks existing callers against a changed callee. The same content ID is idempotent.</p></Card><Card title="The switch is lazy, not global" icon="clock"><p>Nodes refresh app code independently. A resident actor notices a new code pointer on its next call, drops its old instance, and rechecks migrations. There is no fleet-wide stop-the-world cutover or guarantee every actor runs the same version simultaneously.</p></Card></div>
      <Note title="Escape hatches are explicit" tone="amber"><code>--allow-breaking</code> can bypass compatibility refusal. <code>--allow-unresolved-calls</code> can permit unresolved or mismatched callees. Neither flag makes an incompatible protocol safe; coordinate changes and regenerations deliberately.</Note>
      <Detail title="Content identity and verification"><p>The deployment content ID derives from the component hash plus serialized manifest, so a manifest change is significant even with the same Wasm. Nodes verify the fetched component SHA256 against the deployment pointer and manifest. A new pointer increments the deployment version; that number is separate from actor epochs and txids.</p></Detail>
      <Source files="crates/node/src/deploy.rs:93-149, 192-215; crates/node/src/node.rs:271-290; crates/node/src/actor.rs:146-152"/>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Schema evolves with a call" title="Migrations are transactional and append-only.">
      <Flow nodes={[{ title: "Old actor schema", text: "Previous migration set" }, { title: "BEGIN IMMEDIATE", text: "First call on new code" }, { title: "Pending migrations", text: "_statex_migrations" }, { title: "Method + COMMIT", text: "Same transaction", tone: "mint" }, { title: "Same durable segment", text: "Schema + business changes" }]}/>
      <p>A failed first method also rolls back its pending migrations; they are retried on a later successful call. Explicit <code>_create</code> uses a touch operation to apply migrations without running a business method. Never rewrite, reorder, or remove applied migration history; add a new SQL file.</p>
      <Note title="Deploy authorization lives outside StateX" tone="amber">Anyone with write access to the deployment namespace can publish code. Restrict <code>deploy/</code> writes to authorized CI; nodes need read access there plus the appropriate coordination/data permissions elsewhere. Peer HMAC is not deploy or end-user access control.</Note>
    </Section>
    <Section eyebrow="Typed clients in both directions" title="One schema, two kinds of caller.">
      <div className="grid two"><Card title="An external caller" icon="code"><p><code>statex codegen python</code> produces a typed client with a stdlib HTTP runtime. It addresses actors through the public API of any node and maps JSON values into generated language types.</p></Card><Card title="Another actor" icon="branch"><p><code>statex calls sync</code> generates client WIT under <code>wit/deps/</code> and Rust call shims. Methods take the target key first; the runtime dynamically links these imports to NodeCaller.</p></Card></div>
      <Detail title="What to test at each layer"><p>Guest-native tests check business SQL and error handling with a mock host and per-key state. Stub outgoing actor calls explicitly. Runtime tests cover component instantiation, WIT mapping, policy and limits. Cluster tests cover forwarding, graceful handoff, crash recovery, fencing, compaction and deploy compatibility. The e2e script exercises built components, clients, actor calls and workspace checks.</p></Detail>
      <Detail title="Native tests are useful, but not a simulated cluster"><p>The Rust test host gives each (actor type, key) its own in-memory SQLite database. <code>testing::call</code> wraps a closure in a transaction and rolls back on panic; <code>testing::try_call</code> also rolls back on Err. <code>mock_http</code> installs an HTTP handler, generated client <code>stub(...)</code> functions stand in for actor callees, and unstubbed calls panic. These tests validate business logic but do not exercise object-store durability or real network forwarding.</p></Detail>
      <Source files="crates/cli/src/calls.rs; crates/guest/src/testing.rs; sdk/python-guest/statex_testing.py; crates/node/tests/cluster.rs; scripts/e2e.sh"/>
    </Section>
  </>;
}

